"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The preoperative PSA was elevated at 6.5. The surgical findings included a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2), pT2 stage with no extraprostatic extension, positive surgical margins, and no seminal vesicle invasion. Absence of lymph nodes removed is noted, which widens uncertainty regarding nodal status. Biopsy results showed Gleason 3+3 in both sessions, which is consistent with the pathology report.\n\nCountervailing findings include the absence of lymphovascular invasion and the absence of lymph node removal. The biopsy-to-prostatectomy difference is noted, though the final pathology grade was 3+4. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data prevents a definitive assessment of the subsequent trajectory. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trend is crucial for revising this prognosis. The current heuristic sensitivity range suggests a potential recurrence horizon. The fixed estimated horizon is 76.42 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 45.85–106.99 months; its coverage has not been validated."
